	"identifying_description": "Leaf of silk twill with opaque watercolor on paper and sequins. Carved and incised sticks and guards of bone with inlaid cut steel discs and applied silver gilt. Glass jewel and copper alloy rivet.",
	"web_text": "In the 1790s, the influence of Neoclassicism on contemporary women's fashion led to a shift from heavy silk dresses to those of lightweight, typically white, cottons. With less need for fanning, fans decreased in size during this period, remaining small until circa 1810. These small fans, known as imperceptibles, often had mounts made of silk, gauze, or net, and decorated with metallic sequins and painted designs. The blue-and-white designs found on this example evoke the color scheme of the popular jasperware (unglazed, fine-grained stoneware) by Josiah Wedgwood.",
	"didactic_label": "In the 1790s, the influence of Neoclassicism on contemporary women's fashion led to a shift from heavy silk dresses to those of lightweight, typically white, cottons. With less need for fanning, fans decreased in size during this period, remaining small until circa 1810. These small fans, known as imperceptibles, often had mounts made of silk, gauze, or net, and decorated with metallic sequins and painted designs. The blue-and-white designs found on this example evoke the color scheme of the popular jasperware (unglazed, fine-grained stoneware) by Josiah Wedgwood.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 9/25/18 - 7/719\nFans in Fashion, Legion of Honor, 5/23/81 - 8/30/81",
